    "body": "torchrun sets `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1` for every process when `--nproc_per_node` is larger than 1, unless the variable is already set. `DeepSpeedCPUAdam` then steps on a single OpenMP thread, and nothing in the log says so. The deepspeed launcher sets the thread count only with `--bind_cores_to_rank`, which is the path the ZeRO-Offload tutorial documents. Users who launch with torchrun or accelerate get no hint.\n\n**Measured effect**\n\nSetup: ZeRO-1 with `offload_optimizer: cpu`, Qwen3-4B in bf16, fp32 gradient accumulation. The time is for `engine.step()` (CPU Adam plus the copies around it), median over the measured steps.\n\n| Hardware | Launch | `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1` | threads set per rank | Speed-up |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| one GH200, 72-core host | 1 process, variable set to 1 by hand to match the torchrun default | 5.6 s | 0.8 s (36 threads) | 7× |\n| B300 | `torchrun --nproc_per_node 2`, real default | 3.92 s | 1.76 s (32 threads, half a NUMA node per rank) | 2.2× |\n\nOn B300, each rank steps half of the optimizer state, so the single-thread cost is already split over two processes. In a 2-GPU torchrun job on L40S at the default, the same step took 6.2 s.\n\nThe B300 run with threads set also changed the attention path for packed micro-batches. That doesn't touch `engine.step()`, so the optimizer column only reflects the thread count.\n\n**Change**\n- `single_thread_warning()` in `deepspeed/ops/adam/cpu_adam.py` returns a message when `OMP_NUM_THREADS` is `\"1\"` and the rank has at least two cores (CPU affinity divided by `LOCAL_WORLD_SIZE`).\n- `DeepSpeedCPUAdam.__init__` logs it once per process, for the first optimizer instance.\n- The CPU Adam tuning section of the ZeRO-Offload tutorial gets a note for torchrun users.\n\nIt only warns and does not change the thread count:\n- an explicit `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1` may be intentional;\n- the OpenMP runtime may already have read the variable by the time the optimizer is built.\n\n**Tests**\n\n`tests/u